            <ArticleTitle>[Is the negative T-wave on the ECG always a sign of ischemia? (human stress cardiomyopathy?) ].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>210-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 74-year-old woman was admitted on account of chest pain which developed after a death in the family. On the ECG on admission there were negative T waves (the picture of extensive subapicardial ischaemia). Coronarography made on the subsequent day was quite normal. The laboratory finding (CK, CK-MB, AST) did not suggest an acute coronary attack. Ultrasound examination of the heart revealed only slight hypokinesia of the anterior wall. ECG returned to normal within 3 months. Four years later the patient is asymptomatic. The ECG finding and clinical course suggest the clinical entity of &quot;stress cardiomyopathy&quot;, which is not a well known and unequivocally accepted diagnosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
